# Line endings belong to the repository, not to whichever machine checked it out.
#
# Without this file, `core.autocrlf=true` — the Git for Windows default — hands a
# Windows developer CRLF working files while every blob in this repository is LF.
# Git hides the difference, so the tree looks clean and the build looks fine, and
# then anything that reads source text off the disk quietly behaves differently on
# one machine than another. That is not a formatting preference; it is a
# reproducibility defect, and it cost a real debugging session.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises on the way in and checks out LF everywhere. It
# matches the `end_of_line = lf` this repository already declares in
# .editorconfig, so the two now agree instead of only one of them being enforced.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch interpreters are the one genuine exception: cmd.exe can mis-seek
# `goto`/`:label` in a batch file that uses bare LF. Nothing here needs these
# today — they are declared so that the first one added is correct by default.
# PowerShell is deliberately absent: it reads LF correctly.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Never run an end-of-line filter over these. The repository tracks no binary
# file today, which is exactly why the rules belong here now: the first logo,
# font or fixture database someone commits must not be corrupted by a heuristic
# that guessed "text".
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary

# Fixture and backup databases are binary and must survive byte-for-byte; a
# normalised SQLite file is a corrupted SQLite file.
*.sqlite3 binary
*.sqlite  binary
*.db      binary
*.aushbackup binary
